 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Just one reason Antonin Tron’s second collection for Balmain looked better than the first is that we could see it properly. This season’s Paris show was held in the Hôtel de Maisons, Karl Lagerfeld’s former home; as with last season, there was curtain-related production storytelling going on. The windows were covered from the outside with opaque plastic sheeting against which water was lashed as we listened to an extended Jim Morrison mix that started with “Riders on the Storm.” Crucially, the lights stayed high enough for us to pay attention to Tron’s garments.</p><p>This talented designer is working to find his line into the Balmain narrative. That involves relating the Balmain story to his. Tron is a Parisian with a surfer’s soul; this seemed to explain why, along with some great colorized backstage photos of Balmain shows in the 1940s, his cipher this season was philosopher Michel Foucault’s 1975 trip to the West Coast.</p><p>“I read that book called <em>Foucault in California,</em>” Tron said before the show. “It’s about him experimenting with LSD in Death Valley, and basically this idea of the desert as a space for expanding consciousness, experimenting, also disillusion.” From there, he said, came “American desert psychedelicism, Jim Morrison, sexuality, Joan Didion, Walter De Maria”—a collision between the strictures of French intellectual thought and the psychic expansiveness of the American West. Soundtrack apart, the clearest tribute to Morrison came in look 12: laced cowhide pants worn under a powerfully purple eelskin topcoat and an ocelot jacquard shirt.</p><p>There was another strand too. Tron had been looking at Roger Caillois, the French writer and stone collector whose thinking around naturally occurring forms fed into the collection’s colors, embroideries, and surface treatments. “Mankind was not inventing shapes” was how Tron characterized Caillois’s proposition; nature had already produced them. This thinking was most literally reflected in the mineral-patterned wedge heels, and further extended both in the animalier that featured throughout much of the collection and in Tron’s fluid approach to building clothes around the bodies wearing them.</p><p>The upshot was an onrush of sophisticatedly sexy garments that many of the Balmain-wearing women in Lagerfeld’s salon would want to acquire. The sense of déshabillé sparked by those backstage pictures was reflected in loosened-girdle miniskirts, corset-built tops, ruched dresses, and pieces of draped jersey that seemed caught somewhere between being dressed and not-quite-so. There were abbreviated satin bombers over exposed bras, low-rise trousers, soft-shouldered tailoring, and evening dresses whose asymmetric folds and hanging panels looked as if they were still being pinned into place.</p><p>The fabrics were often lush, visually immersive, and borderline trippy. Tron repeatedly built them up through three-stage treatments: Jacquards were printed and then re-embroidered, while one “toxic fish” surface combined sequins, overprinting, and hand-embroidery. Toward the end, that onrush of material substance reached its peak in dresses where animalier patterns were further encrusted with embroidery and crystal. Perhaps it was all the LSD references kicking in, but I had a mild flashback to Peter Dundas’s tenure at Roberto Cavalli.</p><p>This felt like a diligently conceptualized and excellently executed collection of <em>hawt</em> womenswear you could see going down a storm at the Hôtel Costes and other louche-luxe environments. Yet Tron, I suspect, is still working toward the formula that will unlock a free connection between his own creative ego and the shape of the house whose future he is carving. Foucault was fascinated by the <em>épreuve</em>: the test or trial through which truth might be made manifest. Tron’s Balmain odyssey has barely begun, and it is starting to go places.</p></div>",
